+# Decision: Deliver board updates over self-hosted WebSockets
+
+## Context
+
+Editors' committed changes must reach connected staff browsers on a board that serves up to 800 simultaneous connections with opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second. The committed-change delivery route shapes the design of three components — the change store, the delivery layer, and browser sync — so it is chosen once, at the project level, before those nodes are sized.
+
+This decision builds on the existing choice to keep commands over HTTPS with one authoritative event revision per edit: whatever transports changes to browsers, it reads from that single revision stream and never invents its own.
+
+The freshness floor is the stated goal — committed changes visible within 15 seconds under normal connectivity — but subsecond updates were identified as genuinely useful, and no single criterion (operating cost, minimal operational burden) was declared overriding. The tradeoff is therefore a real weighing, not a constraint solution.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Update latency** *(major)* — how soon after commit an edit appears in a connected browser. The 15-second goal is a floor; design that sits comfortably under it is preferred.
+- **Recurring cost** *(moderate)* — fees that scale with connection count and run for the life of the product.
+- **Vendor coupling** *(moderate)* — how much of the board's live data path depends on one provider's SDK, credential model, subscription semantics, and availability.
+- **Owned operational burden** *(moderate)* — connection-capacity planning, reconnection behavior, and publication reliability the project must build and run itself.
+- **Reuse of committed machinery** *(minor)* — the goals already force a retained revision history and recovery after a 20-minute disconnect; an approach that rides the same replay path avoids adding a parallel mechanism.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description:** Browsers run a client-side loop that, every five seconds, fetches board changes newer than the cursor they last saw, over plain HTTPS. The change store indexes revisions for cursor queries; delivery is ordinary request/response endpoints; no connection state exists anywhere. The simplest possible operation: stateless, horizontally scalable, and trivially load-balanced. The cost is latency (changes arrive seconds late, worst case at the poll interval) and repeated reads — every browser re-reads the board whether or not anything changed.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- Update latency: **weak** — seconds of lag by construction, with the worst case one full poll interval and margin.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no fees; the board's own servers serve the reads.
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — none; plain HTTPS the project fully controls.
+- Owned operational burden: **strong** — no connection state, no sessions to manage; capacity is the same request-serving problem the command path already has.
+- Reuse of committed machinery: **strong** — cursor queries read the same retained revision history the recovery goal already requires.
+
+**Summary:** Trades freshness and redundant read load for the simplest stateless operation available.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description:** The project operates its own WebSocket layer. Delivery admits and tracks one socket session per connected browser, recording the revision each session last received; when the command path commits an edit, delivery publishes that revision to every session that should see it. The change store keeps a replayable revision history with reliable publication handoff, so a browser that disconnects and returns fetches exactly the revisions it missed. Browser sync runs reconnection with backoff and catch-up by replay, and keeps a visibly stale indicator while disconnected so a dead socket is never mistaken for a quiet board.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- Update latency: **strong** — push at commit time; latency tracks publication, not a poll interval.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no vendor bill; the cost is servers the project already operates.
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — none; the socket protocol and data model are the project's own.
+- Owned operational burden: **weak** — connection capacity, session tracking, publication reliability, and reconnect handling are all ours to build and run.
+- Reuse of committed machinery: **strong** — the replay path a reconnecting socket needs is the same one the 20-minute-disconnect recovery goal already requires.
+
+**Summary:** Buys low-latency, vendor-independent updates at the price of owning connection capacity and reconnection behavior.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description:** A managed pub/sub provider (Ably- or Pusher-style) owns the connection layer. Delivery wraps the provider's SDK: after the command path commits an edit, delivery publishes it through the provider, and a reconciliation mechanism in the store verifies that every committed revision actually reached the provider. Browsers adopt the provider's subscription and reconnect model instead of a project-built one.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- Update latency: **strong** — managed push networks deliver near-commit, typically faster than self-run sockets under burst.
+- Recurring cost: **weak** — per-connection fees recur at the scale of 800 simultaneous browsers, indefinitely.
+- Vendor coupling: **weak** — the live path's protocol, credentials, and subscription semantics belong to the provider; leaving means rewriting delivery and browser sync.
+- Owned operational burden: **partial** — connection capacity and reconnection mechanics leave our plate, but publication reconciliation on top of the provider is a new reliability surface the other options don't have.
+- Reuse of committed machinery: **weak** — the provider path runs parallel to the retained replay history, and reconciliation adds a layer between them.
+
+**Summary:** The quickest route to low-latency updates with the least connection operations, in exchange for recurring fees and a provider-shaped dependency in the hot path.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets win on the major criterion — update latency — and avoid the two costs that compound over the product's life: recurring fees and vendor coupling. Polling's strength (minimal burden) is real but spends the major criterion to get it; the hosted service's strength (less connection ops) is outweighed here by coupling the live data path to a provider's subscription model and pricing.
+
+The choice's weakness — owned operational burden — is an accepted tradeoff: the project explicitly takes on connection-capacity and reconnect work to keep the board off a realtime vendor. The minor criterion reinforces it: the replay machinery sockets need is already owed to the 20-minute recovery goal, so self-hosting adds no second mechanism where the hosted option would.
+
+Committing obligates the three downstream components: the change store must keep replayable revisions with reliable publication handoff, delivery must manage socket sessions and publication, and browser sync must implement reconnect and catch-up. Nothing is permanently foreclosed — the HTTPS command boundary and the authoritative revision stream are untouched, so polling or a provider could still replace the transport later by rewriting delivery and browser sync only.
